<Base title="Parent guide: first league night & spotting fake cards" description="A plain-English guide for parents: what happens at a Pokémon league night, what to bring, and how to spot counterfeit Pokémon cards and packs.">
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>A parent's guide to card night</h1>
    <p class="lede">You don't need to know the game. Here's what to expect, and how to protect your kid's money from fakes.</p>

    <h2>Your first league night</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Call or check the store page first.</b> Ask if it's a casual night or a tournament, and whether beginners are welcome.</li>
      <li><b>Bring a deck, or ask about learn-to-play decks.</b> Many stores sell or lend simple starter decks. A ready-made "League Battle Deck" or theme deck is fine for a first visit.</li>
      <li><b>Make a free Play! Pokémon account.</b> Official events ask for a Player ID. A parent sets this up through a Pokémon Trainer Club account. It's needed for tournaments, not usually for casual trading.</li>
      <li><b>Plan to stay.</b> Most stores expect younger kids to have an adult present. Weeknight events usually run 1–3 hours.</li>
      <li><b>Know the age groups.</b> Official tournaments split players into Juniors, Seniors, and Masters by birth year, so kids mostly play kids.</li>
      <li><b>Set trade rules ahead of time.</b> For example: "no trading cards worth more than $5 without asking me." Trades between kids can get lopsided fast.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="fakes">How to spot fake Pokémon cards and packs</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Price too good to be true?</b> It probably is. Booster boxes and "rare" cards sold well under the usual price at flea markets or online are a red flag.</li>
      <li><b>Check the pack wrapper.</b> Real packs are sealed cleanly with crisp printing. Blurry art, misspellings, odd fonts, or loose crimps suggest fakes or resealed packs.</li>
      <li><b>Feel and look at the card.</b> Real cards have a layered core. Fakes often feel thin, glossy, or flimsy, and the colors look washed out or too saturated.</li>
      <li><b>Read the text.</b> Spelling mistakes, wrong energy symbols, odd HP numbers (like 1000 HP), or missing copyright lines at the bottom are common giveaways.</li>
      <li><b>Look at the back.</b> Compare it with a card you know is real. Fakes often have the wrong shade of blue or a blurry swirl.</li>
      <li><b>The light test.</b> Many fakes let through much more light than real cards when you hold a phone flashlight behind them. Real cards are not perfectly opaque either, so compare with a known real card.</li>
      <li><b>Buy sealed product from real stores.</b> Local game shops, big-box retailers, and the official Pokémon Center are the safest places. At card shows, buy singles from vendors who let you look closely.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">For high-value cards (roughly $50+), consider buying graded cards from well-known grading companies, or ask a local shop to look at the card before you pay.</p>

    <h2>Card show tips for families</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Set a cash budget before you go in. Many vendors take cards, but cash helps with haggling.</li>
      <li>Go early for the best selection, or late on Sunday for better deals.</li>
      <li>Bring a binder or toploaders to protect cards on the way home.</li>
      <li>Check each show's page for kid-ticket rules. Several Austin-area shows let kids 5 and under in free.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <Signup />
</Base>
